      <h1>Does a Trust Have to Be Notarized?</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Short answer: usually no — but several related documents almost always do. Most states require only that
        the trust instrument be signed by the settlor. The exceptions are Louisiana (witnesses required) and a
        handful of states where state-specific rules apply.
      </p>

      <h2>The General Rule</h2>
      <p>
        Under the Uniform Trust Code, which most states have adopted in some form, a trust is created when the
        settlor has capacity, a valid trust purpose exists, and the trust instrument is executed with the
        formalities state law requires. For a standard revocable living trust, that formality is usually just the
        settlor's signature — a notary's seal is not what makes the trust valid.
      </p>

      <h2>Where You Do Need a Notary</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>The deed transferring real property into the trust.</strong> Virtually every state requires the
          deed to be notarized (acknowledged) before it can be recorded with the county. This is the step people
          most often mistake as a "trust notarization" — it's the deed, not the trust itself.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Certificates of trust for banks and title companies.</strong> Many institutions want the
          certificate in affidavit form with a notarized signature before they'll rely on it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>County-specific practice.</strong> Some recorders reject unnotarized supporting documents even
          when the statute doesn't require it.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2>State Exceptions</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Louisiana</strong> — trusts must be in writing with the signature witnessed by two witnesses
          and notarized (La. R.S. 9:1732). This is the clearest notarization requirement of any state.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Ohio</strong> — trusts holding Ohio real estate are effective only if the trust instrument is
          recorded with the county recorder (R.C. 5801.401). Recording brings its own notarization requirements
          in practice.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Tennessee</strong> — revocable trusts can be created orally if the settlor expresses an intent
          to create one and a trustee holds property, but written trusts follow the general signature rule.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Michigan</strong> — no notary required for validity, but certificates of trust must be in the
          form of an affidavit (MCL § 700.7913), which is typically notarized.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What This Means for Your Documents</h2>
      <p>
        A trust instrument without a notarization block is still valid in most states — but plan for the
        notary anyway. If the trust holds real estate, the deed must be notarized to record. And banks routinely
        ask for a notarized certificate of trust before opening accounts or wiring proceeds.
      </p>

      <h2>Notarizing Documents Generated Here</h2>
